    "result_text": "All tests green. Here's what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC 7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**Root cause:** OfficeFloor's Spring starter (`SpringServerHttpConnection`) hands a handler's return value to Spring MVC's normal return-value machinery. Returning `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` therefore gets Spring's automatic `application/problem+json` content type. The 409 (`DuplicateIdentity`, `CityAtCapacity`) and 429 (`DailyOwnerLimit`) handlers plus bean-validation 400s already did this. But six field-level **400** handlers returned a plain `OwnerFieldErrors` POJO (`{\"errors\":[...]}`), which serialized as `application/json` \u2014 failing the new `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` test (removing `city` \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsException`).\n\n**Changes** (all in `rest/escalation/`):\n- Added a `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail, fields)` overload that produces the standard problem body and adds an `errors` member naming the rejected field(s), preserving the information the old body carried.\n- Converted the six 400 handlers \u2014 `MissingOwnerFields`, `InvalidTelephone`, `InvalidEmail`, `DisposableEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `FutureRegistrationDate` \u2014 to return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` via that helper.\n- Deleted the now-unused `OwnerFieldErrors` class.\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged (every converted handler still responds `400`). Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: I kept the rejected field names as an `errors` array inside the problem body (as an RFC 7807 extension member) rather than dropping them, since that information was part of the create endpoint's contract and the spec only requires the four standard members be *present*, not that they be the only ones. If you'd prefer a bare problem body with no `errors` member, that's a one-line change.",
