        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and improves the codebase's coherence.\n\n- **Right unit of logic.** The common RFC 7807 construction stays centralized in `ProblemDetails`. The new four-arg `build(ex, status, detail, fields)` delegates to the existing three-arg overload and only layers on the `errors` member \u2014 proper reuse, no duplication of the title/timestamp/schemaValidationErrors boilerplate.\n\n- **Converges on the established pattern.** The six field-error handlers now match the exact shape of the pre-existing `ProblemDetail` handlers (`DuplicateIdentityExceptionHandler`, `DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler`): `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>>`, `ProblemDetails.build(...)`, `response.send(ResponseEntity.status(...).body(detail))`. The previously divergent `OwnerFieldErrors` contract is retired, so a maintainer now sees one consistent error-body idiom across all handlers.\n\n- **Dead code removed cleanly.** `OwnerFieldErrors` is deleted and has no remaining references in `src/main/java`.\n\n- **No catch-all growth.** Each handler keeps only its distinct detail message and field name; the shared concern lives in the helper. The overload is a natural, additive extension rather than a grab-bag.\n\nThe per-handler repetition of `ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(detail)` is inherent to the one-class-per-exception OfficeFloor convention and matches the rest of the package, so it's idiomatic rather than a smell."
